Phase 6 defect-injection corpus (D-06-25, D-06-29)
===================================================

This directory holds the held-out `.schway` fixtures the DX-04 defect injectors
(match, move, borrow) operate on, plus one small subject fixture for the
stale-evidence class. It follows testdata/phase5's flat layout: `.schway`
files side by side, no subdirectories.

Two prefixes, two disjoint uses -- do not blur them:

- `heldout_*.schway` -- scored by the CI gate ONLY. These are the fixtures
  session.MatchInjector / MoveInjector / BorrowInjector mutate and the
  fixtures the repair driver (06-13) is verified against. NEVER read these
  files, or their content, when hand-deriving the driver's kind->edit
  mapping.
- `derivation_*.schway` -- the ONLY fixtures anyone may consult when hand-
  deriving the repair driver's kind->edit mapping in 06-13. NEVER wire an
  injector runner or a CI-gate test against these files.

This split (D-06-29) blunts -- it cannot eliminate, given how small this
language is -- the automated-program-repair overfitting failure mode: a
repairer written against the same fixtures it repairs, by the same author,
is not evidence the underlying protocol is agent-legible (D-06-23). 06-13
adds a test asserting no `derivation_*` reference ever names a `heldout_*`
path.

`heldout_match_defect.schway` is this corpus's characteristic marker file: a
later `isPhase6Corpus`-shaped dispatch (06-15, mirroring
`cmd/schway/main.go`'s `isPhase5Corpus`) recognizes this directory by that
file's presence, exactly as `testdata/phase5`'s own marker
(`restrict_borrow.schway`) is recognized today.

Per-file injector targets, marked by a trailing `// schway:<class>-target`
comment on the line the injector mutates (deleted for match, corrupted for
move/borrow -- see session_phase6_injectors.go for the exact mechanism and
the fail-closed marker-count guard every injector shares):

- `heldout_match_defect.schway` / `derivation_match_defect.schway` -- every arm
  self-maps (`Alt => Alt`), so re-adding a self-mapping arm for a removed
  alternative reproduces the file byte-for-byte (D-06-26).
- `heldout_move_defect.schway` / `derivation_move_defect.schway` -- the held-out
  fixture has a three-hop move chain while derivation has two; the injector
  corrupts the marked middle `take`'s source back to the already-moved parameter.
- `heldout_borrow_defect.schway` / `derivation_borrow_defect.schway` -- two
  overlapping shared borrows (legal); held-out also reborrows the first loan
  after the marked second borrow. The injector escalates the marked borrow to
  exclusive, producing a genuine loan conflict.

The cleanup class (D-06-25) needs no dedicated fixture here at all: it
reuses `session.ReleaseOmissionMutationRunner` directly against whatever
`.schway` fixture's generated C already carries a `schway:release-site` marker
(e.g. `testdata/phase4/acquire_three_success.schway`) -- see
`session_phase6_injectors_test.go`.

`stale_evidence_subject.schway` is neither `heldout_` nor `derivation_`: the
stale-evidence class is not a source defect at all (D-06-25), so there is no
kind->edit mapping to overfit and no CI-gate corpus to keep separate from
it. It exists purely as a small, legitimate program to capture an evidence
manifest over before re-touching it.
